  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Osburn was founded in 1887 as a modest trading outpost named for Billy Osburn, a principal investor, and became a logistical hub for early travelers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Incorporated in 1906, Osburn grew rapidly as a hub for silver, lead, and zinc extraction during the late 19th‑century mining boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Silver Valley, where Osburn sits, was long the ancestral homeland of the Schitsu’umsh (Coeur d’Alene) people, who hunted, fished, and gathered across its forests, rivers, and lakes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "At 770 meters high, Osburn lies on rugged terrain shaped by the Osburn Fault, a major geological feature that influenced local ore deposits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By the mid‑20th century, declining ore quality and rising costs closed the mines, turning Osburn into a quieter residential community that still preserves shafts and the fault line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The influx of miners and traders prompted upgrades—mills and housing—that transformed Osburn from a nascent rail stop into a bustling hub amid the mining boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/osburn_idaho",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    }
